the award-winning film nowhere to run  was produced by the Yar'Adua foundation  in Abuja an NGO that focuses on social  issues covering the issue of climate  change from a Nigerian perspective was a  key part of the concept there was very  little awareness about it and there was  definitely no policymaking or any  response to what we thought was a global  threat and we thought that if you had a  documentary that it was in people's  faces and told the story of climate  change from a Nigerian perspective that  he would it would have that that impact  of raising raising awareness the film  shows that Nigeria is one of the  countries most affected by climate  change sea levels are rising while lake  chad is shrinking and desertification is  advancing rapidly the title of the film  nowhere to run highlights the fact there  is no escape from the effects of climate  change in this community over 180  children is affected by Google by  inhaling the crude oil no is gas the gas  flaring in Nigeria is the largest single  contributor to co2 in Africa  only natural absorbance to these  greenhouse gases is the plants that is  the forest  the first part of document is very  depressing but then when you get to the  second part where we begin to highlight  the solutions and people begin to see  that you know there's something you  could do and I think that's what gives  us hope is that there is an opportunity  to respond to the threats that we see  solar and wind power combat Nigeria's  energy shortage and at the same time  provide clean energy that helps prevent  further climate change the producers of  the film say their goal is both  education and advocacy I think what has  been most gratifying to me is the  response from Nigerian audiences they've  begun to make the connection between  climate change and what is already  happening in their local communities  everything from desertification to  flooding to rising sea levels to the  shrinking of Lake Chad in Africa the  expected temperature increase is far  above the global average and many  paradises could soon be lost forever  so producing a film about climate change  from an African perspective is crucial
 

